-- SHADOW-SEMANTICS cell I20 (S1-NS (a), RE-EXPORT ARM SYMMETRY): the method arrives through
-- a chain that re-exports it by METHOD name -- `export import ifc.{size, Box}` -- and names
-- the interface nowhere. Under S1-NS the VALUE arm admits `size` at every re-export depth,
-- so the occurrence is an ordinary dispatch: `impl Sizeable Int` answers 5 * 10 = 50.
--
-- 🚨 GRADED ON BUILD, because that is the only verb that could see this. An implementation
-- whose re-export arm matches INTERFACE names only leaves `size` out of the dispatch-eligible
-- set: the mark pass leaves a plain `EVar`, emit falls into arg-tag dispatch, and `medaka
-- build` fails `E-PANIC: arg-tag dispatch on impl type that owns no constructors` while
-- `check` exits 0 and `run` prints the right answer. That asymmetry was live on this PR and
-- is enumerated NON-CONFORMANT by S1-NS -- the fourth independent namespace guess, at the one
-- site nobody had counted.
--
-- ⚠️ BASELINE, stated so "was live on this PR" is not read as "was broken on main": the
-- MERGE BASE prints 50 here too. This cell guards a regression an intermediate state of that
-- PR introduced -- it pins a shape the ruling makes UNREPRESENTABLE, not a value the ruling
-- changes. Of the three S1-NS cells (i19, d23, i20) only i19 differs from the merge base
-- (777 -> 42); d23 is 42/42 and this one is 50/50 either side.
--
-- The `impl Sizeable Int` is load-bearing: a PRIMITIVE receiver is what turns the missing
-- mark into a panic rather than a silently different route, because a primitive carries no
-- cell tag for arg-tag dispatch to read.
import reexp.{size}

main = println (size 5)
